# ⚠️ THE INTERNAL BUILD LOG AND THE CUSTOMER'S OWN REQUIREMENT CAPTURE.
#
# PLAN.md is a running record of an engagement: it names the tenant, the workspace, the item and
# the account they were deployed under, and it quotes meetings with people at the university by
# name. REQUIREMENTS.md is their description of how their timetabling actually works, written down
# in German as they said it.
#
# Both are genuinely useful and neither is ours to publish. They are also not what a gallery
# template is for — what a reader needs is README.md and AGENTS.md, which are written for them
# rather than for us. `tools/verify_publishable.py` fails if either reaches a staged tree.
PLAN.md
REQUIREMENTS.md

# ⚠️ THE SEMANTIC MODEL IS GENERATOR OUTPUT, AND IT IS ONE LAKEHOUSE'S COORDINATES.
#
# `tools/fabric/build_semantic_model.py` writes every file under `fabric/` — the TMDL, the
# relationships, the measures — and `definition/expressions.tmdl` embeds the OneLake URL of the
# workspace and lakehouse it was built against. A clone would carry a Direct Lake model pointing at
# storage nobody else can reach, which is worse than carrying none: it looks like it should work.
#
# `fabric/CampusInsights.SemanticModel/` is worse again — the archived predecessor's model, whose
# Fabric items were deleted, sharing a `logicalId` with the live one and so confusing Fabric git
# integration on first sync.
#
# The generator ships. Run it against your own workspace and it writes your own model:
#   $env:FABRIC_WORKSPACE_ID = "…"; $env:FABRIC_LAKEHOUSE_ID = "…"
#   python tools/fabric/setup_lakehouse.py; python tools/fabric/build_lakehouse_tables.py
#   python tools/fabric/build_semantic_model.py
fabric/

